  "text": "Questions five and six. What is throughput versus latency in model serving, and how does request batching improve GPU throughput? Latency is one request's time, judged at the tail: the ninety-fifth or ninety-ninth percentile. Throughput is requests per second, across the system. One request leaves most of a GPU idle, and every pass has a fixed cost. In a simple cost model, a fixed cost per pass plus a little per request, a batch of thirty-two gets about eight times the throughput of one at a time, for four times the latency per pass. Past that, throughput flattens and latency keeps climbing. Simulate one GPU on that model, at a thousand requests a second. One at a time, it finishes three hundred and twenty a second, so the line grows by six hundred and eighty every second. Taking whatever has queued, up to thirty-two at once, it keeps up: ninety-nine percent finish within about ten milliseconds. Serving frameworks also wait a few milliseconds to fill a batch. At a hundred requests a second, a five-millisecond wait lifts the median from three milliseconds to eight. At low load, waiting is pure latency, so cap it. And once one GPU's batches are full, add replicas: more throughput, and no replica gets slower.",
